"# History: Atheism, Peer\\-Review and gosh-darn Philosophy\n\\(Written June 23, 2025\\)\nReddit post on r\/AskReddit\n> Atheists of Reddit: What could change your mind?\nhttps:\/\/www\\.reddit\\.com\/r\/AskReddit\/comments\/v09mmi\/comment\/iaf1owu\/\n\nOne person writes the only right answer:\n> Peer\\-reviewed scientific evidence\\.\n\nAnd someone who goes by  `u\/God\\-of\\-Memes2020` replies:\n> This is an incredibly ignorant answer\\. I’m an atheist, but science can only collect data on physical things\\. Most believers think God is immaterial\\. Thus, God’s existence is not a scientific issue\\.\n> \n> You can claim that nothing exists that science can’t test, but then that’s reasoning circularly against religious belief and is ultimately just as dogmatic \\(if not more\\) than theism\\.\n> \n> Edit: downvotes instead of arguments\\. Figures\\. You Reddit “scientism” atheists sound a lot like the believers you hate\\!\n\nThis, I will say upfront, is a stupid argument\\.\nBelievers don’t get to dictate whether or not the tangible existence of a thing is a “scientific issue”\\.\nSo I have things to say:\n> It is not an ignorant answer\\. It is the **only** answer\\.\n> \n> All we can do, as rational human beings, is operate off of evidence and reason\\. That's how our species has accomplished its many wonders: By working with conclusions drawn from data\\. We're not fumbling around in the dark\\.\n> \n> ***Everything*** ​**is a \"scientific issue\"**​, because everything is best approached with rational thinking and methodology\\. *Everything* is a matter of judging what is \"right\" or \"correct\" based on the information available to us\\. Geology and sociology, politics and religion, finance and friendships, ​*everything*​\\.\n> \n> I recognize that theists tend to assert that the nature of God is beyond evidence and \"not a scientific issue\", but ​**we don't have to play by those rules**​\\. We *shouldn't* play by those rules\\.\n> \n> In our world today, we can account for and predict virtually every phenomena, macro or micro, by way of constantly refined mathematical models\\. ​**Our universe consistently follows rules**​\\. So why should we entertain the baseless, arbitrary, and **uselessly unfalsifiable** claim that God doesn't?\n> \n> If everything else is findable through empirical study, exploration and interrogation, why not God?\n> \n> \\-\\-\\-\n> \n> If we are to be convinced of the existence of a God, it will be the same way we are convinced of anything else: ​**Evidence**​\\.\n> \n> \\(EDIT: If they don't have it, if they don't think they need it, if they assert that \"God is immaterial\", that's fine, but the conversation's over at that point\\.\\)\n> \n> So don't be a dick\\.\n\n“We’re not fumbling around in the dark\\!” My favorite\\.\n“Everything is a \"scientific issue\", because everything is best approached with rational thinking and methodology\\. Everything is a matter of judging what is \"right\" or \"correct\" based on the information available to us\\.” Damn straight\\.\n\nSo he comes back with:\n> You’re equating argument and proof with science\\. Logic and math aren’t sciences, nor is philosophy\\. You don’t need science to prove that 2\\+2=4, and science *can’t* prove that sort of thing\\. But it’s true\\.\n\nSo this might be where what I say is a ​*little*​ shaky? But\\.\\.\\. I dunno\\. Maybe not\\. Let’s see\\. I’m reading message by message as I go\\.\n> \"Science\" proves 2\\+2=4 by putting 2 rocks next to 2 other rocks and counting them \\(and then, of course, having countless experts around the world review the procedure, try to disprove the conclusion, and find as many edge cases as they can for the remainder of human history\\)\\.\n> \n> That's the evidence\\. That's the proof\\. That's the logic, the math, and the science\\. That's the Scientific Method, our empirical approach to understanding the world around us, and it's all we have\\. I can't help feeling that your argument around the word \"science\" is ultimately semantic\\.\n> \n> My point still stands: **It does not matter** if the theist claims that God is \"immaterial\" and \"not a scientific issue\"\\. It does not matter that ***they*** argue that evidence is impossible\\.\n> \n> It does not change that fact that the way to convince someone of something, ​*like an atheist of the existence of God*​, is **exclusively with evidence** \\(which is, by its nature, subject to scientific inquiry and peer\\-review\\)\\. If you don't have it, the conversation's over\\.\n> \n> This is true ​**whether you think un\\-evidencable things exist or not**​\\. It's not \"circular reasoning\" or \"dogmatic\", it's cutting out the crap we can't do anything with\\.\n> \n> So I do not understand:\n> \n> Why you think that \"evidence\" isn't a valid answer to \"What would it take to convince you X is true?\"\n> How \"Most believers think God is immaterial\" is at all relevant to a question about atheist perspectives\n\nNah, I still like this\\.\n\nHe comes back with:\n> Counting two rocks and two more rocks and concluding that there are therefore four rocks is what need to be proved, not evidence\\.\n> \n> Let me put this another way\\. Do you know of any scientists saying, in a peer reviewed article in a good journal, that science has proven God doesn’t exist? Neil De Gras Tyson or Frodo Harris saying some cannoli on Twitter isn’t automatically a scientific claim\\.\n> \n> By “science” I mean, yes, the scientific method, a key part of which is the analysis of data to see whether it proves or disproves the hypothesis, data that does not and cannot exist for an immaterial God\\.\n> \n> Here’s an old thread from r\/math you might find interesting, if you’re open to considering the possibility that you’re wrong: https:\/\/www\\.reddit\\.com\/r\/math\/comments\/25f70a\/does\\_the\\_study\\_of\\_pure\\_mathematics\\_use\\_the\/\n\nHe’s lost me\\.\nI’m\\.\\.\\. I’m pretty sure this is all bullcannoli that he’s saying\\.\nThe rocks are the evidence, and you can’t prove a negative\\.\nData does not and cannot exist for an immaterial god, but an immaterial god is a\\) itself an arbitrary claim and b\\) by its nature irrelevant to our physical world\\.\n\nSo what do I do here\\.\\.\\.\n> Let's refocus, then\\. I still do not understand the root of your thread:\n> \n> Why do you think that \"evidence\" isn't a valid answer to \"What would it take to convince you X is true?\"\n> How is \"Most believers think God is immaterial\" is at all relevant to a question about atheist perspectives?\n> \n> We can debate what is and isn't science all day, we can ponder \"can science prove an immaterial God?\" all day, but that's all extraneous to the question and answer we're responding to\\.\n> \n> It would take evidence to convince me of the existence of a God\\. Either theists can provide it or they can't, but the reason they can or cannot doesn't matter\\.\n> \n> All we have is evidence\\. We can't do anything without it\\.\n\nI guess I\\.\\.\\. try to pull us back from the brink and re\\-emphasize what I feel is the center here and get him to answer my damn questions\\.\n\nSo here’s where we get into philosophy, I think\nHe responds\n> Oh, I have no problem with evidence at all\\. Basically all I’m trying to point out in this thread is that there is unquestionably other types of evidence \\(logical, deductive arguments, for example\\) that have nothing to do with science in the sense of ultimately being reducible to physical matter\\. And I also am trying to point out that it’s not obvious that only physical things exist, e\\.g\\., “quaila” in philosophy \\(https:\/\/plato\\.stanford\\.edu\/entries\/qualia\/, see especially section 3\\)\\. Mathematical figures like squares and straight lines are also good contenders for real non\\-physical things, since perfectly straight lines and perfect squares don’t exist in nature\\. That’s really all I’m trying to say\\.\n\nSo\nOkay\nI think\nComing back to this 3 years later\nI think he’s arguing that evidence for the existence of a god would not come from scientific, peer reviewed evidence, as it leaves no physical trace \\- but rather, perhaps, from something like logic or mathematics, which can “prove” things in a separate way from the scientific method and the physical world\\.\nWhich\\.\\.\\. \nWell, honestly?\nI still think that needs peer review, and I think the study of such a thing, the exploration of it, would constitute “evidence”\\.\n\nBut how did I respond then?\n> I'm glad to hear that, and I think I understand what you're getting at and where you're coming from\\. It seems to me that your main point, across all your arguments about philosophy and evidence across this thread, is: \"Respect any kind of evidence, not just 'scientific'\\.\"\n> \n> And in principle, I agree with that\\.\n> \n> But I also think your arguments are needlessly broad, and you're **needlessly harsh** on those narrowing things down\\. I take issue with you calling the specificity of \"scientific\" an \"incredibly ignorant answer\", because it's not\\. It's a practical one\\.\n> \n> We are pondering the existence of a hypothetical entity who exists \\(in some capacity\\) in nature and has a tangible effect on our physical world\\. That's an objective question about the nature of the universe, and the physical phenomena therein\\.\n> \n> Physical evidence is all that we have to understand our physical universe\\. Mathematics and logic are tools we use to work with universe\\-related evidence, but they do not independently provide it\\.\n> \n> Beyond that, we can speculate about non\\-physical evidence, we can speculate about no evidence, but all of that is kind of moot, isn't it? If our goal is hard evidence, of any kind, we're not going to find it in speculation\\.\n> \n> I understand that your perspective is one of philosophy, that you don't want to rule out any of the fascinating possibilities and approaches, and I respect that\\. But the approach I \\(and the \"Peer\\-reviewed scientific evidence\" guy\\) take to this question isn't philosophy\\. It's cold, hard physical evidence\\. It's \"Show me\"\\.\n> \n> And there's nothing wrong or ignorant about that, because it's all we've got right now when it comes to asking objective questions about the universe\\. Everything else is just noise\\.\n\nAnd he says\n> By saying or implying that the only evidence one would accept to start believing in God, people are implying that a deductively valid argument with true premises wouldn’t suffice, which is absurd and “incredibly ignorant\\.” Their idea of “proof” and “evidence” needs to be broadened, which is what I’m trying to do\\.\n> \n> Logic and math are potentially about things, like numbers, principles, and argument forms, that exist outside the physical universe, which is where God could be\\. They are tools to study the universe, but that are not only tools for that\\.\n> \n> God could exist outside the physical universe, so attempting to prove him with physical evidence or maintaining that only physical evidence could prove it is unfair to religious believers\\. It is reasoning circularly, or “begging the question,” a logical fallacy, because no one except an atheist would accept that premise\\. You need to already think that everything is physical to believe that science is the only form of proof\\.\n> \n> You and peer\\-reviewed evidence guy are trying to use the naked eye to disprove the existence of germs or atoms\\. \\(Atoms, btw, were proven with arguments back in Ancient Greece, not microscopes\\. And yes, some philosophers didn’t buy those arguments: but they should have\\. It’s nice that science can help confirm the conclusions of those arguments, but it’s not strictly needed to prove atoms or something like them as a subsisting material for change\\.\\)\n> \n> And again, for the fifth or sixth time in this thread, I’m an atheist\\!\n\nI think there’s no reason to believe there’s any kind of logical or mathematical argument for the existence of god\\. I think the religious certainly don’t have one\\. I think, after thousands of years, someone would have come up with something\\. I think there’s no physical evidence, and one would expect a god who physically impacts the world to have physical evidence \\- in fact, there are plenty of examples in “apologetics” of trying to argue physical evidence\\. And I think anything outside of that is hand\\-waving speculation about “what if there’s a whole other thing?”\n\nIf there is a real, personal god \\- a sapient entity who defines and affects our world and who you can have a personal relationship with \\- that is a question of\\.\\.\\. hmm\\. I feel like I almost have it but I can’t figure out what to say\\.\n\nBut I think it’s fair to treat the absence of both correct logic and physical evidence as reason enough to dismiss, I think it’s totally fair to ask for physical evidence of the existence of a god to whom we attribute our physical universe, and I think it’s totally fair to ask for the exploration and peer review of some magical line of logic that proves the existence of such a being\\.\n\n\\(Oh, also \\- “atoms and germs were proven with logic, not microscopes”\\. Were they? Were they proven? Or were they just reasonable arguments that could have easily been countered by other parallel arguments, but those ones happened to be right? What were the counter arguments, and why, from a logical perspective and disregarding any scientific evidence, were they counter arguments wrong and the atom arguments right? And what does that mean \\- atoms were proven with logic? Did they logic put protons, electrons, neutrons, atomic weights, covalent bonds, etc\\.? Or did they just say “probably there’s building blocks”?\\)\n\nAnyway\nWhat did I say\\.\\.\\.\nSomething with nested bullet points, so I won’t be block\\-quoting all of this one\\.\n\n> I guess all I can really say here is:\n\n- The \"true premises\" need to come from somewhere\\.\n- There's a lot of hypotheticals and speculation here: \"​**potentially**​ about things\\.\\.\\.\", \"where God ***could*** be\", \"​***could***​ exist outside\\.\\.\\.\", etc\\.\n\t- Again, I respect your goal of accounting for all possibilities, but I can't do anything with what\\-ifs\\.\n- I still agree with you that other kinds of evidence, if solid, are perfectly valid, but I'd rather focus in on the physical stuff as a ​**practical heuristic**​\\.\n\t- I do not assert that God himself is necessarily physical, but that ​**here, today, the only evidence available to us would be**​\\.\n- None of this is unfair to religious believers, because it isn't about them\\.\n\t- It's about what it would take to make a skeptic a believer, something that is not owed to anyone\\.\n- That \"naked eye\" sentence is a hell of a leap\\.\n\n> EDIT, for wrap\\-up purposes: The reality here is that we're approaching this question from two very different perspectives, disciplines and, perhaps, rule\\-sets\\. And that's fine\\. I get where you're coming from, I understand what your approach is\\.\n> \n> I just think you're coming down too hard on people for focusing their attention on what is, today, within the context of what we know, our most likely avenue for that kind of evidence\\. And I think all of us, you, me, and peer\\-reviewed evidence guy, all agree that at the end of the day, whatever its nature, evidence is important and necessary\\.\n\nAnd then he\\.\\.\\. oh god the ontological argument\n> I’m going to bed my friend but you’ve earned my respect for keeping at it this long\\.\n> \n> My wife Lol’d her ass off that her atheist husband spent the night on Reddit defending the possibility of God\\.\n> \n> Edit: Here’s one more shot: https:\/\/iep\\.utm\\.edu\/anselm\\-ontological\\-argument\/ \\(section 3\\)\\. Premise 3 is horsecannoli but if something like this came out with all true premises, I’d start believing in God\\. Case closed\\. I’d then argue that that God is a gosh-darn prick though\\! Would you not? Is that argument insufficient because it isn’t scientific, if one with all true premises came around?\n\nAnd my final reply:\n> Have a good night\\. It's been a spirited and interesting discussion\\.\n> \n> EDIT: Oh no, not the ontological argument\\.\n> \n> But yes, such a hypothetical argument would be sufficient\\. I don't deny the validity of a solid logical argument\\. I just figure that, thousands of years in, someone would have found it by now, so it's not where I place my attention\\.\n\n\\(I don’t think he ever saw my edit\\.\\)\n\nSo honestly, I think I got most of the stuff I’m spitballing here in 2025 in the original thing\\.\n\n---\n\nOh wait wait wait there’s other stuff from that post\\.\n\nTo the initial peer review thing, someone replies\n> Peer\\-reviewed scientific evidence of what exactly? It seems this a general moving goal post where no amount of anything would persuade you\\.\n> \n> We see it things outside of religion where someone says something like “I’d believe XYZ if there were peer\\-reviewed scientific evidence for it\\.” If it is produce people always say “that doesn’t count because ABC\\.”\n\nWell, no, buddy, that’s not what this is\\.\nYou’re just assuming there will bo goalpost moving\\.\n\nI reply:\n> Evidence of the existence of a God, to be subjected to scrutiny and interrogation via the Scientific Method, same as literally anything else\\.\n> \n> The reason the Scientific Method works, the reason it's given us the ability to go to the moon, carry the world's collective knowledge in our pocket, and cure smallpox, is because countless experts around the world constantly experiment and collect data, then scrutinize and interrogate and poke holes and cut away the crap, ​**until nothing but unassailable facts remain**​\\. That's the whole point\\.\n> \n> The Scientific Method is not people pretending to be experts and moving goalposts in order to argue forever\\. It's the methodical refinement by crucible of our understanding of the universe, performed by a planet's worth of individuals sharing their work with each other\\.\n> \n> It does not matter what the nature of the evidence is, so long as it is genuinely strong evidence for the asserted phenomena\\. What matters is that it withstands scrutiny\\.\n\nHe replies:\n> This is a non\\-answer\\. If asked fir what sort of evidence I’d need for “literally anything else” I’d be able to give a general answer\\. For example if someone asked what sort of evidence would be needed to prove Relativity I’d say how time is perceivable different for objects in orbit\\. If someone asked for evidence that vaccines work I’d be able to say how mortality rates drop\\. But with the question of God no one can say what sort of missing evidence they’d expect to find\\.\n> \n> It’s like a reverse falsifiability test where you propose a question where there isn’t a theoretical answer which would satisfy\\.\n\nI reply:\n> I do not personally have any ideas for evidence or tests of the existence of God\\.\n> \n> ​**That doesn't invalidate the need for evidence**​\\. It does not make such a request a bad\\-faith response or a \"non\\-answer\"\\. It does not imply a refusal to be persuaded or the moving of goalposts\\.\n> \n> It doesn't make \"evidence\" any less of a rational, clear\\-cut answer to the question of \"what would it take to change your understanding of the world\"? \"Evidence\" is the *only* answer\\.\n> \n> **It does not matter** if anyone can think of what that evidence might look like\\. **It does not matter** if that evidence is even *possible* or not\\.\n> \n> If you want to change someone's understanding of something, you provide them with evidence\\. If you can't, sorry, conversation's over\\. Without evidence, one story is just as good as another\\.\n\nStill very happy with how I was writing all this stuff 3, 4 years ago\\. I think I did well, just in general, in my writing\\.\n\nHe replies and I don’t respond:\n> I can understand the argument and agree up to a point\\. But the issue is the reason why it is so rare to get a straight answer out of skeptical critics is because all too often they don’t even understand what they’re rejecting\\. I can’t say what I’d expect to find if Hinduism were false, not because “tHeRe iS nO eViDeNcE” but because I’m not informed enough in Hinduism to evaluate their beliefs\\.\n\nI don’t need to understand the details of a belief system to reject a supernatural claim\\.\nI don’t need a pilot’s license to recognize the problem when I see a helicopter in a tree\\.\n\nSomeone else, who I downvoted, writes in response to the initial “Peer\\-reviewed scientific evidence of what exactly?”:\n> It's a lazy answer where they can offload all of the work to this nameless, faceless \"science\" rather than actually doing any kind of work themselves\\. It's the same intellectually lazy answer as \"I don't know but a god would\"\\.\nFlying Spaghetti Monster, guys\n\nOh, I guess I wrote a direct reply to peer\\-review guy:\n> I just want to say that I'm sorry to see so many people taking issue with your use of the words \"peer\\-reviewed scientific\", because \"Evidence\" is the final, and only, answer to the question being asked, and encompasses just about all the other answers here\\.\n\nI just\nAnti\\-science, anti\\-intellectualism\\.\\.\\. it’s\\.\\.\\. sad and frustrating\\."
